    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pick\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12 (!at least latest Nightly build!)\n\n### Description\n\nI have configured ripgrep (rg) as per their official documentation, I have a conf file where I am setting --follow so I can open symlinks.\nBut the builtin picker shows nothing. Running the same search from CLI works just fine, meaning that the config file is reading properly. \nAdditionally using builtin.cli() and passing rg and the pattern works, which it means it uses the configuration from the file. But builtin.files, builtin.grep and builtin.grep_live seems to ignore it.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n1. Set the variable for rg to find the configuration file path, i.e. `export RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH=$HOME/.config/ripgrep/rg.conf`\n2. Add a single line to the file with `--follow`\n3. Navigate to a directory that contains only links to other directories and/or files.\n4. Do a search from CLI using rg\n5. Ensure there are results showing (it should use the setting --follow set before)\n6. Do the same from Neovim when using Mini.Pick\n7. No results are shown at all.",
